\\mathfrak {q}}\\) of K lying over q. We conclude because by [cite53†4 , Lemma C.1.7 and its proof] we have\nL192: $$\\begin{aligned} v_\\ell (h_i)-e_{\\ell , i}= v_\\ell \\left( \\frac{[K(\\zeta _{T_\\ell \\ell ^{t_\\ell }}, W^{1/\\ell ^{t_\\ell }}):K(\\zeta _{T_\\ell \\ell ^{t_\\ell }}, W_{\\ne i}^{1/{\\ell ^{t_\\ell }}})]}{[K(\\zeta _{\\ell ^{t_\\ell }}, W^{1/\\ell ^{t_\\ell }}):K(\\zeta _{\\ell ^{t_\\ell }}, W_{\\ne i}^{1/{\\ell ^{t_\\ell }}})]}\\right) . \\end{aligned}$$\nL193: \nL194: \\(\\square \\)\nL195: With the notation of Proposition cite48†3.2 we could have \\(e_{\\ell , i}< v_\\ell (h_i)\\): indeed, for \\(K={\\mathbb {Q}}\\) and \\(W=\\langle 5 \\rangle \\) we have \\(Sep_2={\\mathbb {Z}}_{\\geqslant 0}\\) and \\(Sep=2\\, {\\mathbb {Z}}_{>0}\\).\nL196: ### Lemma 3.3\nL197: \nL198: Let \\(\\alpha _1, \\ldots , \\alpha _r \\in K^{\\times }\\) be multiplicatively independent. There exist a positive integer N and \\(S \\subset ({\\mathbb {Z}}/N{\\mathbb {Z}})^{r+1}\\) such that for \\((z_0, \\ldots , z_r)\\in {\\mathbb {Z}}_{>0}^{r+1}\\) with \\(lcm (z_0, \\ldots , z_r)= z_0\\) the following holds: given \\((x_0, \\ldots , x_r)\\in {\\mathbb {Z}}_{>0}^{r+1},\\) there is\nL199: $$\\begin{aligned} \\sigma \\in Gal \\left( F\\left( \\zeta _{z_0}, \\alpha _1^{1/z_1}, \\ldots , \\alpha _r^{1/z_r}\\right) /K\\right) \\end{aligned}$$\nL200: such that \\(\\sigma |_F \\in C\\), \\(\\sigma \\left( \\zeta _{z_0}\\right) = \\zeta _{z_0}^{x_0},\\) and \\(\\sigma \\left( \\alpha _i^{1/z_i}\\right) = \\zeta _{z_i}^{x_i}\\alpha _i^{1/z_i}\\) for all \\(i=1,\\ldots , r\\) if and only if \\(\\gcd (x_0, z_0) = 1\\) and \\((x_0 \\bmod {N}, \\ldots , x_r \\bmod {N}) \\in S\\). The integer N only depends on K, F and \\(\\langle \\alpha _1, \\ldots , \\alpha _r\\rangle ,\\) and N, S are computable with an explicit finite procedure.\nL201: Supposing that the prime divisors of \\(z_0, \\ldots , z_r\\) belong to some set D,  we may remove from N all prime factors not in D.\nL202: ### Proof\nL203: \nL204: The condition \\(\\gcd (x_0, z_0) = 1\\) is clearly necessary, so assume that it holds. Calling \\(W=\\langle \\alpha _1, \\ldots , \\alpha _r\\rangle \\), by Proposition cite49†3.1 there is some positive integer N such that \\(F\\cap K(\\zeta _\\infty , W^{1/\\infty })\\subset K(\\zeta _N, W^{1/N})\\) and such that for every positive multiple M of N we have\nL205: \nL206: $$\\begin{aligned} \\left[ K\\left( \\zeta _M, W^{1/M}\\right) : K\\left( \\zeta _N, W^{1/N}\\right) \\right] =(M/N)^r \\cdot \\varphi (M)/\\varphi (N). \\end{aligned}$$\nL207: Thus we may replace \\(z_i\\) by \\(\\gcd (z_i,N)\\) and work in \\(Gal (K(\\zeta _N, W^{1/N})/K)\\): if one finds a suitable automorphism of \\(K(\\zeta _N, W^{1/N})\\), then one may extend it freely to \\(K(\\zeta _M, W^{1/M})\\) as the degree of the extension above is maximal. \\(\\square \\)\nL208: ### Remark 3.4\nL209: By [cite51†1 , Definitions 5 and 10], elements \\(\\alpha _1,\\ldots , \\alpha _r\\in K^\\times \\) must be strongly \\(\\ell \\)-independent if for all \\(i=1,\\ldots , r\\) there is some prime \\({\\mathfrak {q}}_i\\) of K such that \\(\\ell \\not \\mid v_{\\mathfrak q_i}(\\alpha _i)\\) and \\(\\ell \\mid v_{{\\mathfrak {q}}_i}(\\alpha _j)\\) for all \\(j\\ne i\\). Indeed, if \\(\\alpha :=\\prod \\alpha _{i}^{z_i}\\) holds for some integers \\(z_i\\) and w.l.o.g.\nL210: \\(\\ell \\not \\mid z_1\\), then \\(\\ell \\not \\mid v_{{\\mathfrak {q}}_1}(\\alpha )\\) hence \\(\\alpha \\) is strongly \\(\\ell \\)-indivisible. Similarly, if the \\(\\alpha _i\\)’s are not units and their norms \\(N_{K/{\\mathbb {Q}}}(\\alpha _i)\\) are pairwise coprime and not of the form \\(\\pm 1\\) times an \\(\\ell \\)th power, then they are an \\(\\ell \\)-good basis (as in [cite51†1 , Theorem 14]) for \\(\\langle \\alpha _1,\\ldots , \\alpha _r \\rangle \\).\nL211: ### 3.1 Kummer extensions contained in cyclotomic extensions\nL212: Consider a prime power \\(\\ell ^e\\mid \\tau \\) and a cyclic Kummer extension L/K of degree \\(\\ell ^e\\) with \\(L\\subset K(\\zeta _\\infty )\\). Call \\(K'\\) the largest subfield of \\(K\\cap {\\mathbb {Q}}(\\zeta _\\infty )\\) of degree a power of \\(\\ell \\) (over \\({\\mathbb {Q}}\\)). We then have \\(L=L'K\\) for some unique \\(K'\\subset L'\\subset {\\mathbb {Q}}(\\zeta _\\infty )\\) such that \\([L':{\\mathbb {Q}}]\\) is a power of \\(\\ell \\) (and hence \\([L': K']=\\ell ^e\\)).\nL213: We have \\(L' \\subset {\\mathbb {Q}}(\\zeta _n)\\) for some positive integer n of the form\nL214: $$\\begin{aligned} n = \\ell ^{T+e}Q\\quad \\text {where}\\quad Q=\\prod _{q \\equiv 1\\! \\pmod {\\ell }} q\\quad \\text {and}\\quad \\zeta _{\\ell ^T}\\in K(\\zeta _Q), \\end{aligned}$$\nL215: where q denotes a prime. Indeed, if \\(L' \\subset {\\mathbb {Q}}(\\zeta _n)\\) and q is a prime divisor of n with \\(q \\not \\equiv 0, 1 \\pmod {\\ell }\\), then \\(L' \\subset {\\mathbb {Q}}(\\zeta _{n/q})\\) and hence we may replace n by n/q; the same holds if \\(q \\equiv 1 \\pmod {\\ell }\\) and \\(q^2 \\mid n\\). Notice that the extension \\(L'/K'\\) corresponds to a cyclic quotient of order \\(\\ell ^e\\) of \\(G:=Gal ({\\mathbb {Q}}(\\zeta _n)/K')\\).\nL216: ### Proposition 3.5\nL217: \nL218: Replace G by its largest quotient of exponent \\(\\ell ^e\\) and suppose that \\(G \\simeq \\prod _{i} {\\mathbb {Z}}/\\ell ^{e_i}{\\mathbb {Z}}\\). The i-th factor corresponds to a cyclic Kummer extension \\(K'(\\gamma _i)/K'\\) of degree \\(\\ell ^{e_i}\\) for some \\(\\gamma _i\\in {\\mathbb {Q}}(\\zeta _n)\\) such that \\(\\gamma _i^{\\ell ^{e_i}}\\in K'\\).\nL219: \nL220:   1. (i)\nL221: We have \\(L'=K'(\\gamma )\\) for some \\(\\gamma :=\\prod _{i} \\gamma _i^{y_i},\\) where \\(0\\leqslant y_i < \\ell ^{e_i}\\) and for some i we have \\(e_i=e\\) and \\(\\ell \\not \\mid y_i\\).\nL222: \nL223:   2. (ii)\nL224: \nL225: For every \\(q\\mid n\\) such that \\(q\\not \\mid \\ell \\Omega \\) fix an algebraic integer \\(g_q\\in {\\mathbb {Q}}(\\zeta _{\\ell ^e q})\\) such that \\(g_q^{\\ell ^e}\\in {\\mathbb {Q}}(\\zeta _{\\ell ^e})\\) and such that\nL226: $$\\begin{aligned} v_\\ell ([{\\mathbb {Q}}(\\zeta _{\\ell ^e}, g_q):\\mathbb Q(\\zeta _{\\ell ^e})])=\\varepsilon _q\\quad \\text {where}\\quad \\varepsilon _q:=\\min (e, v_\\ell (q-1)). \\end{aligned}$$\nL227: Notice that \\(g_q\\) depends on \\(q,\\ell ^e\\) but not on K and L. For every \\(q\\mid n\\) we may fix \\(\\gamma _i\\) as follows: if \\(q\\not \\mid \\ell \\Omega ,\\) then \\(\\gamma _i^{\\ell ^{e_i}}=g_q^{\\ell ^{\\varepsilon _q}},\\) else \\(\\gamma _i\\) is an algebraic integer in \\({\\mathbb {Q}}(\\zeta _{\\ell ^e\\Omega })\\).\nL228: Then \\(\\ell \\not \\mid v_{\\mathfrak {p}} (\\gamma _i^{\\ell ^{e_i}})\\) holds for a prime \\({\\mathfrak {p}}\\) of K only if \\({\\mathfrak {p}}\\) lies over q or over a divisor of \\(\\ell \\Omega \\) respectively, and in the former case there is such \\({\\mathfrak {p}}\\).\nL229: ### Proof\nL230: Since the extensions \\(K'(\\gamma _i)/K'\\) are linearly disjoint, \\(\\prod _{i} \\gamma _i^{z_i}\\in K'\\) implies \\(\\ell ^{e_i}\\mid z_i\\) for every i. Let \\(0\\leqslant y_i,Y_i < \\ell ^{e_i}\\) and suppose that for some \\(i_1,i_2\\) we have \\(e_{i_1}=e_{i_2}=e\\) and \\(\\ell \\not \\mid y_{i_1}, Y_{i_2}\\).\nL231: Then \\(\\prod _{i} \\gamma _i^{y_i}\\) and \\(\\prod _{i} \\gamma _i^{Y_i}\\) generate the same extension if and only if \\(Y_i\\equiv ty_i \\pmod {\\ell ^{e_i}}\\) holds for every i and for some t coprime to \\(\\ell \\), in other words if \\(y_I\\) and \\(Y_I\\) generate the same cyclic subgroup of G of order \\(\\ell ^e\\). By Pontryagin duality the number of those subgroups is the same as the number of cyclic quotients of G of order \\(\\ell ^e\\) and hence (i) follows.\nL232: For the first assertion of (ii) it suffices to choose the isomorphism of G respecting the decomposition \\(Gal (\\mathbb Q(\\zeta _n)/K')=Gal ({\\mathbb {Q}}(\\zeta _{n_1})/K')\\times Gal (\\mathbb Q(\\zeta _{n_2})/{\\mathbb {Q}})\\), where \\(n=n_1n_2\\) and \\(n_2\\) consists of all prime factors \\(q\\not \\mid \\ell \\Omega \\). We then conclude by a result on valuations [cite53†4 , Lemma C.1.7 and its proof]. \\(\\square \\)\nL233: ### Example 3.6\nL234: \nL235: As an aside remark, notice that L may not be contained in the compositum of K, \\({\\mathbb {Q}}(\\zeta _{\\ell ^\\infty })\\), and for any prime q subextensions of \\({\\mathbb {Q}}(\\zeta _q)/K\\cap \\mathbb Q(\\zeta _q)\\) of degree dividing \\(\\ell ^e\\): consider \\(\\ell ^e=2\\), \\(K={\\mathbb {Q}}(\\sqrt{65})\\), and the quartic cyclic field \\(L=\\mathbb Q(\\sqrt{65+8\\sqrt{65}})\\subset {\\mathbb {Q}}(\\zeta _{65})\\).\nL236: ## 4 The Master Theorem on Artin type problems\nL237: \nL238: For \\(h_I\\in {\\mathbb {Z}}_{>0}^n\\), \\(h:=lcm (h_1,\\ldots , h_n)\\), and a positive squarefree integer Q, we write\nL239: \nL240: $$\\begin{aligned} K_Q := K\\left( \\zeta _{Qh}, W_1^{1/Qh_1}, \\ldots , W_n^{1/Qh_n}\\right) \\end{aligned}$$\nL241: and we let \\(C_Q \\subset Gal \\big (FK_Q/K_1\\big )\\) consist of the automorphisms whose restriction to F lies in C, and which, for any choice of \\(q\\mid Q\\) and \\(i\\in I\\), is not the identity on \\(K(\\zeta _{qh_i}, W_i^{1/qh_i})\\). For every set \\({\\mathcal {S}}\\) of primes of K we write \\({\\mathcal {S}}(x):=| \\{{\\mathfrak {p}}\\in \\mathcal S : N{\\mathfrak {p}} \\le x\\}|\\) and\nL242: \nL243: $$\\begin{aligned} \\pi _K(x) := |\\{{\\mathfrak {p}} : N{\\mathfrak {p}} \\le x\\}|= x/\\log x + o(x/\\log x). \\end{aligned}$$\nL244: The density of \\({\\mathcal {S}}\\) is defined by\nL245: \nL246: $$\\begin{aligned} d({\\mathcal {S}}) = \\lim _{x \\rightarrow \\infty } \\frac{{\\mathcal {S}}(x)}{\\pi _K(x)}, \\end{aligned}$$\nL247: \nL248: where it is possible that the limit does not exist.\nL249: ### Theorem 4.1\nL250: \nL251: Assume GRH. Fix \\(h_I\\in {\\mathbb {Z}}_{>0}^n,\\) and let S be the set of primes \\({\\mathfrak {p}}\\) in the domain of \\(\\Psi \\) (restricting \\(\\Psi \\) to the primes of K that are not ramified over \\({\\mathbb {Q}},\\) do not ramify in F,  and do not lie over a prime divisor of \\(\\tau )\\) for which\nL252: \nL253: $$\\begin{aligned} \\left( \\frac{F/K}{{\\mathfrak {p}}}\\right) \\subset C \\quad \\text {and} \\quad \\Psi (\\mathfrak {p})=h_I. \\end{aligned}$$\nL254: The set S has a natural density d(S),  which is 0 if and only if \\(S=\\emptyset \\) if and only if \\(C_Q = \\emptyset \\) for some positive squarefree integer Q. Writing \\(Q_t:=\\prod _{q\\leqslant t} q,\\) we have\nL255: \nL256: $$\\begin{aligned} d(S) = \\lim _{t \\rightarrow \\infty } d_{Q_t},\\quad \\text {where}\\quad d_{Q_t}:=\\frac{|C_{Q_t}|}{[FK_{Q_t} : K]}. \\end{aligned}$$\nL257: The property \\(\\Psi (\\mathfrak {p})=h_I\\) means that \\({\\mathfrak {p}}\\) splits in \\(K(\\zeta _{h_i}, W_i^{1/h_i})\\) and it does not split in \\(K(\\zeta _{qh_i}, W_i^{1/qh_i})\\), for any choice of \\(i\\in I\\) and of a prime q. Requiring the above conditions only for \\(q\\leqslant t\\), we obtain a larger set with natural density \\(d_{Q_t}\\). Theorem cite35†4.1 says that these larger sets are good approximations of S for t large.\nL258: ### Proof\nL259: \nL260: For Q a squarefree positive integer, we have \\(S\\subset S_Q\\), where\nL261: \nL262: $$\\begin{aligned} S_Q:=\\Big \\{{\\mathfrak {p}} \\;: \\!\\! \\quad \\left( \\frac{FK_Q/K}{{\\mathfrak {p}}}\\right) \\subset C_Q \\Big \\}. \\end{aligned}$$\nL263: \nL264: By the Chebotarev density theorem the natural density of \\(S_Q\\) is \\(d_Q:={|C_{Q}|}/{[FK_{Q} : K]}\\). We have \\(d_Q=0\\) if and only if \\(C_Q=\\emptyset \\), and these conditions imply \\(S=\\emptyset \\).\nL265: \nL266: We claim that for any positive integer t we have\nL267: $$\\begin{aligned} S_{Q_t}(x) -S(x) \\leqslant O(\\pi _K(x)/t) + o(x/\\log x), \\end{aligned}$$\nL268: \nL269: (1)\nL270: \nL271: where the implied constants depend on \\(n, K, F, W, h_I\\). We deduce that d(S) exists and (as \\(t\\mapsto d_{Q_t}\\) is non-increasing) it equals \\(\\inf \\{d_{Q_t}\\}\\).\nL272: Let \\(Q_0\\) be the squarefree part of the constant c from Proposition cite49†3.1 (iii), assuming \\(n! h\\mid c\\) and the additional condition involving F. We suppose \\(C_{Q_0} \\ne \\emptyset \\) and prove \\(\\inf \\{d_{Q_t}\\}>0\\). Let \\(L_q:=K(\\zeta _q, W^{1/q})\\) and \\(L_{q,i}:=K(\\zeta _q, W_i^{1/q})\\). Since \\([L_{q,i}:K]\\geqslant (q-1)q\\) and by linear disjointness, for \\(t\\geqslant Q_0\\) we have\nL273: $$\\begin{aligned} d_{Q_t} = d_{Q_0} \\prod _{q\\leqslant t, q \\not \\mid Q_0} \\frac{|Gal (L_q/K)\\setminus \\bigcup _{i\\in I} Gal \\left( L_q/L_{q,i})\\right) |}{|Gal (L_q/K)|} \\geqslant d_{Q_0} \\prod _{q > n}\\left( 1 - \\frac{n}{q(q-1)}\\right) . \\end{aligned}$$\nL274: The product over primes \\(q > n\\) is strictly positive: It suffices to show that the product over \\(n< q < y\\) is bounded from below independently of y. Taking the logarithm of the partial products and using the bound \\(\\log (1 - \\alpha ) \\geqslant -2\\alpha \\) (valid for \\(0 \\leqslant \\alpha \\leqslant 1/2\\)) we have\nL275: $$\\begin{aligned} \\prod _{n< q< y} \\left( 1 - \\frac{n}{q(q-1)}\\right) = \\exp \\left( \\sum _{n< q< y} \\log \\left( 1 - \\frac{n}{q(q-1)}\\right) \\right) \\geqslant \\exp \\left( -\\sum _{n< q < y} \\frac{2n}{q(q-1)}\\right) , \\end{aligned}$$\nL276: \nL277: which is bounded from below by \\(\\exp (-2n\\zeta (2)) = \\exp (-\\pi ^2 n/3) > 0\\). Hence \\(d_{Q_t}\\) is bounded from below by a positive constant independent of t, as \\(d_{Q_0} > 0\\).\nL278: We are left to prove the claim (cite54†1 ). For t large enough, every \\(q>t\\) satisfies \\(q\\not \\mid h\\) and \\([L_{q,i}: K] \\geqslant (q-1)q\\). Setting \\(f_1(x) := \\sqrt{x}/(\\log x)^{100}\\) and \\(f_2(x) := \\sqrt{x}(\\log x)^{100}\\), consider the intervals\nL279: \nL280: $$\\begin{aligned} I_1:=(t, f_1(x))\\quad I_2:=[f_1(x), f_2(x)] \\quad I_3:=(f_2(x) , x] \\end{aligned}$$\nL281: \nL282: and the corresponding sets\nL283: $$\\begin{aligned} \\Gamma _j:=\\{{\\mathfrak {p}} : N{\\mathfrak {p}} \\leqslant x \\text { and }{\\mathfrak {p}} \\text { splits in } L_{q,i} \\text { for some }i\\in I \\text { and }q\\in I_j\\}. \\end{aligned}$$\nL284: \nL285: Any \\({\\mathfrak {p}}\\in S_{Q_t}\\setminus S\\) splits in some \\(L_{q,i}\\) with \\(q>t\\), and \\(N{\\mathfrak {p}} \\leqslant x\\) implies \\(q\\leqslant x\\). Thus \\(S_{Q_t}(x) -S(x) = O\\left( \\left| \\Gamma _1\\right| + \\left| \\Gamma _2\\right| + \\left| \\Gamma _3\\right| \\right) \\). We clearly have\nL286: $$\\begin{aligned} \\left| \\Gamma _j\\right| \\leqslant \\sum _{i\\in I} \\sum _{q \\in I_j} \\left| \\lbrace {\\mathfrak {p}} : N{\\mathfrak {p}} \\le x \\text { and } {\\mathfrak {p}} \\text { splits in } L_{q,i} \\rbrace \\right| . \\end{aligned}$$\nL287: \nL288: By the effective Chebotarev density theorem under GRH [cite55†8 , cite56†19 ] we get\nL289: $$\\begin{aligned}{} & {} \\left| \\Gamma _1\\right| \\leqslant \\sum _{i\\in I} \\sum _{q \\in I_1} \\left( \\frac{\\pi _K(x)}{q(q-1)} + O\\left( \\sqrt{x}(\\log x)^{10}\\right) \\right) \\\\{} & {} \\quad =O\\left( \\pi _K(x)/t\\right) + o(x/\\log x). \\end{aligned}$$\nL290: \nL291: Moreover, considering separately the primes of K of degree greater than 1 we have\nL292: $$\\begin{aligned} \\left| \\Gamma _2\\right| \\leqslant n \\sum _{q \\in I_2} \\left| \\lbrace {\\mathfrak {p}} : N{\\mathfrak {p}} \\leqslant x, {\\mathfrak {p}} \\text { is of degree } 1 \\text { and } {\\mathfrak {p}} \\text { splits in } K\\left( \\zeta _q\\right) \\rbrace \\right| + n[K : {\\mathbb {Q}}]\\sqrt{x} \\end{aligned}$$\nL293: \nL294: and hence by the Brun–Titchmarsh inequality\nL295: $$\\begin{aligned} \\left| \\Gamma _2\\right|&\\leqslant O\\big ( \\sum _{q \\in I_2} \\left| \\lbrace p : p \\leqslant x \\text { and } {p}\\equiv 1\\!\\!\\!\\! \\pmod q \\rbrace \\right| \\big )+ n[K : {\\mathbb {Q}}]\\sqrt{x}\\\\&\\leqslant O\\left( \\sum _{q \\in I_2}\\frac{2\\pi (x)}{q-1}\\right) +o(x/\\log x)=o(x/\\log x), \\end{aligned}$$\nL296: where the last equality follows e.g. by decomposing \\(I_2\\) into dyadic intervals of the form [y, 2y], and bounding the contribution of such intervals by the prime number theorem and the estimate \\(1/(q-1) = O(1/y)\\) for \\(q \\in [y, 2y]\\).\nL297: \nL298: Fixing some \\(w_i\\in W_i\\setminus K^\\times _{{{\\,\\textrm{tors}\\,}}}\\) we have\nL299: $$\\begin{aligned} \\Gamma _3 \\subset \\bigcup _{i\\in I} \\left\\{ {\\mathfrak {p}} : N{\\mathfrak {p}} \\le x \\text { and }{\\mathfrak {p}} \\text { splits in } K\\left( \\zeta _q, w_i^{1/q}\\right) \\text { for some }q\\in I_3\\right\\} . \\end{aligned}$$\nL300: \nL301: Thus \\(\\mathfrak {p}\\in \\Gamma _3\\) implies \\(w_i^{(N{\\mathfrak {p}} - 1)/q} \\equiv 1 \\pmod {{\\mathfrak {p}}}\\) for some \\(i\\in I\\), \\(q \\in I_3\\). So we have\nL302: $$\\begin{aligned} \\prod _{\\mathfrak {p}\\in \\Gamma _3}N{\\mathfrak {p}}\\mid \\prod _{i\\in I} N_{K/\\mathbb Q}\\left( \\prod _{k \\leqslant x/f_2(x)} (w_i^k - 1)\\right) \\leqslant 2^{O((x/f_2(x))^2)} \\end{aligned}$$\nL303: \nL304: (the implied constant also depends on \\(w_i\\)) and hence \\(\\left| \\Gamma _3\\right| = o(x/\\log x)\\). \\(\\square \\)\nL305: ### Remark 4.2\nL306: \nL307: Assuming GRH, d(S) also exists if we replace \\(h_I\\) by some non-empty set \\(H\\subset {\\mathbb {Z}}_{>0}^n\\), and we have\nL308: \nL309: $$\\begin{aligned} d(S(H))=\\sum _{h_I\\in H} d(S(h_I)). \\end{aligned}$$\nL310: \nL311: (2)\nL312: \nL313: For the existence of d(S(H)), let \\(K_C\\) denote the primes \\({\\mathfrak {p}}\\) of K with \\(\\left( \\frac{F/K}{{\\mathfrak {p}}}\\right) \\subset C\\), let \\(d^\\pm \\) denote the upper/lower density and let \\(B(r) := \\{1, \\ldots , r\\}^n\\). We have\nL314: $$\\begin{aligned} d_-(\\Psi ^{-1}H \\cap K_C)&\\geqslant \\lim _{r \\rightarrow \\infty } \\sum _{h \\in H \\cap B(r)} d_-(\\Psi ^{-1}\\{h\\} \\cap K_C) = \\lim _{r \\rightarrow \\infty } \\sum _{h \\in H \\cap B(r)} d^+(\\Psi ^{-1}\\{h\\} \\cap K_C)\\\\&\\geqslant d^+(\\Psi ^{-1} H \\cap K_C) - \\lim _{r \\rightarrow \\infty } d^+(\\Psi ^{-1} ({\\mathbb {Z}}_{>0}^n \\setminus B(r))) = d^+(\\Psi ^{-1}H \\cap K_C) \\end{aligned}$$\nL315: by Theorem cite35†4.1 and because \\(d^-(\\Psi ^{-1}B(r))\\rightarrow 1\\). The last claim holds since it is well-known that under GRH for \\(a\\in {\\mathbb {Q}}\\setminus \\{0, \\pm 1\\}\\) the density of the primes p such that \\(Ind _p(a) \\leqslant c\\) exists and tends to 1 as \\(c \\rightarrow \\infty \\), and the same holds for number fields following Hooley’s proof [cite29†5 , Theorem 4.1, especially (3)], see also [cite57†2 , Theorem 4] or [cite58†20 , Theorem 5.1].\nL316: Finally, (cite59†2 ) follows from the observations above because, as \\(r \\rightarrow \\infty \\), we have\nL317: \nL318: $$\\begin{aligned} d(S(H)) = \\sum _{h \\in H \\cap B(r)} d(S(h)) + o(1). \\end{aligned}$$\nL319: \nL320: In the following result we work with \\(\\Psi _T\\) for any squarefree integer \\(T\\geqslant 2\\). An image for this map is a tuple of images of \\(\\Psi _\\ell \\) for \\(\\ell \\mid T\\) prime. To ease notation we describe an element of \\(\\Psi _T\\) as a matrix whose entries are non-negative integers \\(e_{i,\\ell }\\).\nL321: ### Corollary 4.3\nL322: \nL323: Assume GRH for (i).\nL324: \nL325:   1. (i)\nL326: \nL327: Let \\(h_I\\in {\\mathbb {Z}}_{>0}^n\\) and \\(h:=lcm (h_1,\\ldots , h_n)\\). We have \\(h_I\\in Im (\\Psi )\\) if and only if for every positive squarefree integer Q there is an automorphism in\nL328: \nL329: $$\\begin{aligned} Gal \\big (K\\big (\\zeta _{Qh},W_1^{1/Qh_1}, \\ldots , W_n^{1/Qh_n}\\big )/K\\big (\\zeta _{h},W_1^{1/h_1}, \\ldots , W_n^{1/h_n}\\big )\\big ) \\end{aligned}$$\n"
